## 7. REGISTERED OPEN QUESTION — THE NUMERICS LEVER

R2 fixes **self-similarity**, a structural defect in the triple. Whether any single level converges is
a **separate** question and this clause records it so it is not lost. It is not an alternative to R2.

A2's D6RF10 measured, in the dafoam team's own territory:

| rung | configuration | result |
|---|---|---|
| R1 | `DARhoSimpleFoam`, **nNonOrth 3**, relax_p 0.30, endTime 2500 | 1.681e-05 = **1.681x above floor**, `GATE FAIL` |
| R3 | **SIMPLEC + nNonOrth 12 + relax_p 0.70** | binding field **PASS** with a measured plateau |

**A3GC runs SIMPLEC with `nNonOrthogonalCorrectors 0` and p relaxation 1.0**, i.e. one of the three
components of A2's winning configuration and neither of the other two — and it applies `nNonOrth 0` to
a mesh whose maximum non-orthogonality is **72.07°** (A3GC L2, `meshgen/logCheckMesh.txt`), against
A2's R1 which `GATE FAIL`ed at nNonOrth 3. A2 also measured "just run longer" and found it
**insufficient**; A3GC's endTime 6000 is that same lever.

**Registered levers, with that provenance: `nNonOrthogonalCorrectors` and the p relaxation factor.**
Note the countervailing evidence already in hand: the §0.1 anchor converges to p = 3.744e-07 with
`nNonOrth 0` on this same geometry, so `nNonOrth 0` is demonstrably *sufficient* at r = 1.1674. That
argues the numerics lever is a **remedy for a badly-stretched mesh** rather than a defect in the
settings — which is why R2 changes the mesh and holds the settings byte-identical to the validated
tutorial. Any R2 level that fails §4.4 despite r = 1.1674 re-opens this clause.

---

